findings.body:
`newResetToken()` is a password-reset secret generator. Replacing `randomBytes(32)` (256-bit CSPRNG) with `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)` makes tokens both **predictable** (`Math.random` is not a CSPRNG) and **brute-forceable** (at most \(10^{12}\) values, ~9 base-36 chars after `padStart`).

Any attacker who can request resets or observe token-shaped values can search that space far faster than the prior 64-hex-char tokens allowed. Edge/runtime compatibility does not require this tradeoff: Web Crypto `crypto.getRandomValues` is available on edge runtimes and preserves the original entropy contract.

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```
